line  what's going to work best for them why  do you think is that black women and  other women of color  tend to have these emotional  entanglements when it comes to  negotiating the salary like why are we  so  prone to lowballing ourselves yeah i  love that you use the word entanglement  um because i think that many of us are  really in these workplace entanglements  and i think that as women as black women  we've been loyal to a fault  right because we've been taught you know  just go in work hard keep your head down  if you work hard you're gonna get the  thing right and what we realize is  there's a system in place that doesn't  always reward  those who work the hardest and so i  think because we've been  conditioned to think that's how it works  that this is a meritocracy so it's hard  to unlearn  those things right and so we really have  to lean into that courage and  and ask for what we want and it's small  acts of courage right you know i can  talk to you about this now  but it took a lot to get to this point  right and i still  have to self-talk and have a growth  mindset to say  you know even when i say wow am i asking  for too much there is no too much  because the one thing i want to  stress is there's a range for a reason  right there's a  reason and most companies are going to  expect for you to negotiate in some way  shape or form  right so when you negotiate and they're  like oh well good  you know so so lean into it and do it  not just for you but do it for the  next generation of women of color  because the more we ask we can close  that gap for them  wow that's that's actually a really good  point i never realized  that the range was there for that reason  for negotiating  that's really good feedback um another  really good audience question  if you're familiar how does the us rank  against  other countries in paying black women  yeah you know i don't know the answer to  to that but i imagine i mean from  some of the women that have read the  memo and i hear from they say that hey  we experience the same inequalities  you know in uk and london in south  africa and so  i think globally this is an opportunity  for us to rise up together and say you  know  we're not giving companies we're not  giving anyone pass to pay us  uh unfairly right and so when we talk  about psychological safety part of  psychological safety  is being able to get paid fairly right  doing  work and getting paid comparably and so  i think that  if we want to create that for black  women in the workplace then we need to  look at  the salaries right so if  a woman say has been in the company for  two and a half three years  been busting their butt to you know  bend over backwards as you said you know  it's not always about  how hard you work i think it's it's a  thing of like us learning to work  smarter but let's say  we've been working really hard for a  long time and then it comes to a point  where you ask for a raise  and you don't get it what are what  should you consider  before potentially walking away like  what other things can you do  in that role if you really love it  before you say i've  given it my entirety and now i have to  go  um great question i think that part of  that is under  before you even go into the negotiation  you need to draw your lines in the sand  right you have to practice what are a  few different scenarios  if it's a yes great but if it's a no  then okay not right now  then when right so um go play with those  scenarios or write it down whatever  works best for you  but then understand what you're willing  what you're willing  to say yes to and no to because  oftentimes i think as women as black  women and  black women we center everybody else in  the workplace  and remember that we can also center  ourselves and we have careers  and we have goals and we have dreams so  we have to make sure that we're that  we're  positioning ourselves in the right way  and so the last thing that i'll say  is it's really 